{"name":"Horizon Island","hex":"#cdd4c6","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"horizon","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The visible horizontal line (in all directions) where the sky appears to meet the earth in the distance.","The range or limit of one's knowledge, experience or interest; a boundary or threshold."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/horizon"},{"term":"island","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A contiguous area of land, smaller than a continent, totally surrounded by water.","A contiguous area of land, smaller than a continent, partially surrounded by water; a peninsula; a half-island."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To surround with water; make into an island.","To set, dot (as if) with islands."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/island"}],"entries":[{"title":"Horizon","blocks":[{"text":"Most commonly, the horizon is the border between the surface of a celestial body and its sky when viewed from the perspective of an observer on or above the surface of the celestial body. This concept is further refined as:"}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Horizons.svg/330px-Horizons.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Horizon","wikidata":"Q43261"},{"title":"Island","blocks":[{"text":"An island or isle is a piece of land, distinct from a continent, completely surrounded by water. There are continental islands, which were formed by being split from a continent by plate tectonics, and oceanic islands, which have never been part of a continent. Oceanic islands can be formed from volcanic activity, grow into atolls from coral reefs, and form from sediment along shorelines, creating barrier islands. River islands can also form from sediment and debris in rivers. Artificial islands are those made by humans, including small rocky outcroppings built out of lagoons and large-scale land reclamation projects used for development."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/6b/Hawaje-NoRedLine.jpg/330px-Hawaje-NoRedLine.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Island","wikidata":"Q23442"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/A_colorful_view_of_airglow_layers_at_Earth%27s_horizon.jpg/500px-A_colorful_view_of_airglow_layers_at_Earth%27s_horizon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/A_colorful_view_of_airglow_layers_at_Earth%27s_horizon.jpg/1280px-A_colorful_view_of_airglow_layers_at_Earth%27s_horizon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/37/A_colorful_view_of_airglow_layers_at_Earth%27s_horizon.jpg/500px-A_colorful_view_of_airglow_layers_at_Earth%27s_horizon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The curvature of Earth's horizon as observed from a Space Shuttle at an altitude of 226 km (140 mi).","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AA_colorful_view_of_airglow_layers_at_Earth's_horizon.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Paris_moderne._Les_Tuileries%2C_le_Louvre%2C_et_la_rue_de_Rivoli%2C_vue_prise_du_Jardin_des_Tuileries.jpg/500px-Paris_moderne._Les_Tuileries%2C_le_Louvre%2C_et_la_rue_de_Rivoli%2C_vue_prise_du_Jardin_des_Tuileries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/11/Paris_moderne._Les_Tuileries%2C_le_Louvre%2C_et_la_rue_de_Rivoli%2C_vue_prise_du_Jardin_des_Tuileries.jpg/1280px-Paris_moderne._Les_Tuileries%2C_le_Louvre%2C_et_la_rue_de_Rivoli%2C_vue_prise_du_Jardin_des_Tuileries.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumb